   "item": {
    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Plasma Membrane",
    "stem": "A researcher studies a plasma membrane protein whose polypeptide chain crosses the membrane exactly once. Its amino acid sequence has three consecutive regions: Region 1 (about 100 amino acids) has mostly polar and charged R groups, Region 2 (about 20 amino acids) has almost entirely nonpolar R groups, and Region 3 (about 80 amino acids) has mostly polar and charged R groups. Based on the structure of the phospholipid bilayer, which statement best describes how this protein is positioned in the membrane?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Regions 1 and 3 lie within the bilayer interior, while Region 2 is exposed to the aqueous fluid outside the cell.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reverses the bilayer's layout, treating the membrane interior as hydrophilic because the phosphate heads are charged. The heads face the water; the interior is made of hydrophobic fatty acid tails, which cannot stably surround charged and polar R groups. Fix: Sketch the bilayer first, label heads outward and tails inward, then match each protein region to the environment it resembles chemically."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The entire protein sits on the outer surface of the membrane, bound to the phosphate heads, because proteins are hydrophilic molecules.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This assumes all membrane proteins are hydrophilic and ignores the long nonpolar Region 2 and the stated single membrane crossing. Embedded proteins can be hydrophilic, hydrophobic, or both, and are oriented so each part matches its surroundings. Fix: Check the R-group chemistry of each region and ask whether any segment is nonpolar enough to sit among the fatty acid tails."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Region 2 lies within the bilayer interior, while Regions 1 and 3 both extend into the extracellular fluid on the same side of the membrane.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This correctly places the hydrophobic segment but fails to trace the chain's path. A chain that crosses the bilayer only once must enter from one side and exit on the other, so the regions flanking the crossing cannot finish on the same side. Fix: Trace the polypeptide from one end to the other and count each crossing to determine which side every hydrophilic region ends on."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Region 2 lies within the bilayer interior, while Regions 1 and 3 extend into the aqueous fluids on opposite sides of the membrane.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Phospholipid tails form a hydrophobic core, so the nonpolar R groups of Region 2 are stable there. The polar and charged Regions 1 and 3 interact with water and hydrophilic heads, and because the chain crosses only once, the segments before and after the crossing must end up on opposite sides."
     }
    ]
   },
